    <body>
      <sec id="article-120608.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease is a rare hypertrophic gastropathy characterized by giant mucosal folds predominantly involving the gastric body and fundus; reduced gastric acid secretion; and protein-losing enteropathy, leading to hypoalbuminemia. The disorder is associated with excessive transforming growth factor &#x003b1; signaling and epidermal growth factor receptor activation, resulting in foveolar hyperplasia and glandular atrophy. In adults, M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease typically follows a progressive course and carries an increased risk of gastric adenocarcinoma. In children, the condition is usually acute and self-limited and has been linked to cytomegalovirus infection.&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Helicobacter pylori</italic> infection has also been implicated, particularly in adults. Clinical manifestations include epigastric pain, nausea, vomiting, peripheral edema, weight loss, and anemia.</p>
        <p>Diagnosis is based on endoscopic visualization of enlarged gastric folds, histopathologic confirmation, and exclusion of mimicking conditions. Management focuses on supportive care, treatment of underlying infections when present, acid suppression, nutritional support, and targeted interventions, such as epidermal growth factor receptor inhibitor administration, in refractory cases. Complications include severe protein malnutrition and gastric malignancy, with prognosis dependent on age of onset and response to therapy.</p>
        <p>This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to&#x000a0;improve learners' competence in evaluating and managing M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease. Participants will advance their mastery of the condition's etiology, risk factors, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, and evidence-based diagnostic and therapeutic approaches.&#x000a0;Greater skills will equip clinicians to collaborate with interprofessional teams providing care for affected individuals.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Evaluate signs, symptoms, and diagnostic results consistent with M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease, with attention to risk factors and etiologic contributors.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement personalized, evidence-based&#x000a0;strategies&#x000a0;for managing M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease&#x000a0;and mitigating its potential complications.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Improve patient awareness regarding disease progression, potential complications, and adherence to treatment plans for M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with the interprofessional team to educate, treat, and monitor patients with&#x000a0;M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease to improve&#x000a0;overall health outcomes.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=120608&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=120608">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-120608.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease is a rare disorder characterized by giant mucosal folds in the proximal stomach, reduced acid secretion, and protein-losing enteropathy with resulting hypoalbuminemia. The distal stomach is typically spared. Alternative terms include "hypoproteinemic hypertrophic gastropathy" and "giant hypertrophic gastritis."<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r1">[1]</xref> The condition predominantly affects men but also occurs in women and children. In adults, M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease frequently follows a progressive course and is considered premalignant. The etiopathogenesis is incompletely understood.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r2">[2]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-120608.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>The exact etiology of M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease is unclear. The condition is generally considered acquired rather than inherited, although rare familial cases suggest a genetic contribution.&#x000a0;A report described a large 4-generation family affected by M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease, in which a dominant 1244_1247delACAG alteration in <italic toggle="yes">SMAD4 (</italic>a deletion of the adenine&#x02013;cytosine&#x02013;adenine&#x02013;guanine sequence from nucleotide positions 1244 through 1247, resulting in a frameshift mutation), previously linked to juvenile polyposis syndrome, was identified as a likely causative factor.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>A self-limited variant occurs in children and is associated with cytomegalovirus (CMV) infection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r5">[5]</xref> A similar variant has been reported in adults in association with <italic toggle="yes">Helicobacter pylori</italic> infection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r8">[8]</xref> Overexpression of transforming growth factor &#x003b1; (TGF-&#x003b1;) in the superficial gastric epithelium has been hypothesized to contribute to the pathogenesis of M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease in the context of these infections.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r9">[9]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-120608.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>The prevalence of M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease is unknown, with fewer than 1,000 cases reported to date. The condition affects men more frequently than women. Onset typically occurs in adults aged 30 years or older, with a median age of symptom presentation of 55.&#x000a0;Cases in children have also been documented.&#x000a0;A study reported 5- and 10-year survival rates of 72.7% and 65%, respectively. Gastric cancer developed in 8.9% of patients after 10 years of follow-up.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r10">[10]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-120608.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Increased epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) signaling is thought to result from elevated TGF-&#x003b1; production, which promotes proliferation of mucosal epithelial cells. TGF-&#x003b1;, a ligand that activates EGFR, stimulates epithelial cell growth, leading to abundant mucus production.&#x000a0;TGF-&#x003b1; reduces acid secretion directly via actions on parietal cells and indirectly via stimulation of somatostatin release. Hypersecretion of mucus impairs absorption of nutrients, electrolytes, and vitamins in the small bowel. Disruption of tight junctions between gastric epithelial cells contributes to malabsorption of essential nutrients, resulting in protein-losing gastropathy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r11">[11]</xref> Overexpression of TGF-&#x003b1; also explains hypochlorhydria despite normal or slightly elevated gastric acid levels.</p>
        <p>CMV infection is believed to trigger TGF-&#x003b1; overexpression, thereby activating the EGFR signaling pathway. Excessive TGF-&#x003b1; accumulation in the gastric mucosa may result from viral injury.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r12">[12]</xref> In M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease associated with <italic toggle="yes">H pylori</italic>, the pathogenesis similarly involves EGFR pathway activation. Overexpression of TGF-&#x003b1;, demonstrated in M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease, reproduces the phenotype in transgenic mice. <italic toggle="yes">H pylori</italic> upregulates the pathway and induces TGF-&#x003b1; overexpression through a mechanism distinct from CMV.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r13">[13]</xref> The relationship between <italic toggle="yes">H pylori</italic> infection and the EGFR&#x02013;TGF-&#x003b1; axis is incompletely understood.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-120608.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>The definitive diagnosis of M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease requires endoscopic biopsy. Mucosal biopsies may appear nonspecific or normal.&#x000a0;However, characteristic histopathologic findings include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Foveolar hyperplasia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Oxyntic gland atrophy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Reduction in parietal (acid-producing) and chief (pepsinogen-producing) cells</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Increased eosinophils and plasma cells</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Edema and smooth muscle hyperplasia in the lamina propria</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cystic dilation or corkscrew morphology of epithelial pits</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Preservation of overall mucosal architecture&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r14">[14]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>When mucosal biopsies are nondiagnostic but clinical suspicion for M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease remains, deeper gastric biopsies, including the muscularis mucosae, are indicated to identify the findings listed above.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r15">[15]</xref> Deeper samples&#x000a0;may be obtained using jumbo forceps, polypectomy snares, or endoscopic mucosal resection, often eliminating the need for surgical full-thickness biopsies.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-120608.s7" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease typically has a subtle onset with progressive manifestations in adults. The most common clinical features include epigastric pain (65%), fatigue (60%), anorexia (45%), weight loss (45%), edema (38%), and vomiting (38%).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r16">[16]</xref> Additional symptoms include nausea, diarrhea, and gastrointestinal bleeding secondary to gastric erosion or ulcers.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r17">[17]</xref> Rarely, gastroduodenal intussusception may occur.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r18">[18]</xref> Clinical features resulting from hypoalbuminemia due to protein-losing enteropathy, such as ascites, pleural effusion, and pericardial effusion, may also be observed.</p>
        <p>In children, M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease presents acutely and typically resolves spontaneously within several weeks. A history of CMV infection is often reported in pediatric cases, whereas adults may have a history of <italic toggle="yes">H pylori</italic> infection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r19">[19]</xref> Rare associations with other infections, including <italic toggle="yes">Mycoplasma pneumoniae</italic>, herpes simplex virus, and <italic toggle="yes">Giardia lamblia</italic>, occur more frequently in children than in adults. A weak association with ulcerative colitis has also been reported.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r20">[20]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-120608.s8" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Pierre M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier compared the macroscopic appearance of the pathognomonic thickened gastric mucosa in the disease to cerebral convolutions (gyri). Definitive diagnosis requires endoscopic biopsies, although additional evaluations are often performed before or alongside endoscopy.</p>
        <p>A barium gastrointestinal series demonstrates marked enlargement of gastric rugal folds along the greater curvature, with excessive mucus secretion diluting barium and impairing mucosal coating. Contrast-enhanced computed tomography shows diffuse gastric mucosal thickening.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r21">[21]</xref> Rarely, M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease presents as gastroduodenal intussusception.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r22">[22]</xref> Positron emission tomography&#x02013;computed tomography may reveal increased 18F-fluorodeoxyglucose uptake in thickened mucosa due to inflammation, warranting inclusion of M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease in the differential diagnosis of abnormal gastric&#x000a0;18F-fluorodeoxyglucose accumulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r23">[23]</xref></p>
        <p>Laboratory evaluation should include a complete blood count with differential, complete metabolic panel, ferritin, and gastrin levels. Optional measurements include &#x003b1;1-antitrypsin, fibrinogen, and ceruloplasmin. Assessment&#x000a0;for <italic toggle="yes">H pylori</italic> should be performed using endoscopic biopsy, stool antigen detection, or breath testing. CMV evaluation should include serologic antibody testing and endoscopic biopsies for viral inclusion bodies, with concurrent immunohistochemistry and polymerase chain reaction analysis of biopsy specimens. Typical laboratory findings include hypoalbuminemia (a hallmark), lymphopenia, normal or mildly elevated gastrin, iron deficiency, and reduced levels of &#x003b1;1-antitrypsin, cholesterol, fibrinogen, and ceruloplasmin.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r24">[24]</xref></p>
        <p>Endoscopy reveals giant gastric folds, superficial punctate erosions, and erythema of the proximal gastric mucosa. Endoscopic mucosal or deeper biopsies are required for diagnostic confirmation, as described in the <bold>Histopathology</bold> section. Patients often exhibit hypochlorhydria with relatively normal serum gastrin.&#x000a0;Gastric fluid collection during endoscopy for pH measurement is recommended when available.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r25">[25]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-120608.s9" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Clinical trials evaluating treatments for M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease are limited. Pharmacologic therapies have demonstrated inconsistent efficacy.</p>
        <p>Supportive treatments include a high-protein diet, proton pump inhibitors, and micronutrient replacement. Albumin infusions may be necessary for patients with significant hypoalbuminemia.</p>
        <p>Treatment of <italic toggle="yes">H pylori</italic> and CMV infections is indicated when tests are positive. Eradication of <italic toggle="yes">H pylori</italic> has been shown to improve disease in some patients. Similarly, ganciclovir therapy is effective in certain cases associated with CMV.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r26">[26]</xref></p>
        <p>Octreotide, a somatostatin analog, has demonstrated benefit in case reports. The agent modulates the TGF-&#x003b1;&#x02013;EGFR signaling pathway underlying the pathogenic mechanism. Reported dosing ranges from 100 to 600 &#x000b5;g daily, subcutaneously or intravenously, or 10 to 60 mg intramuscularly every 4 weeks using the depot formulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r27">[27]</xref></p>
        <p>Cetuximab was evaluated in a small phase I, single-arm, open-label case series in patients with severe M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease treated for 1 month. Treatment produced significant improvement in quality of life and biochemical parameters. Cetuximab, a recombinant monoclonal antibody of the immunoglobulin G1 subclass, binds the extracellular portion of EGFR, thereby inhibiting TGF-&#x003b1; binding. To date, this single clinical trial represents the only formal evaluation of pharmacologic therapy in M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease. Dosing consists of an initial loading dose of&#x000a0;intravenous cetuximab (400 mg/m&#x000b2; body surface area), followed by 3 weekly intravenous infusions of 250 mg/m&#x000b2; for 12 months.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r28">[28]</xref></p>
        <p>Surgery is indicated in refractory cases with disease progression accompanied by severe abdominal pain, weight loss, bleeding, or pyloric obstruction. Partial or total gastrectomy is a valid option for patients with persistent, debilitating symptoms or increased risk of gastric cancer. Total gastrectomy is preferred over partial resection due to superior surgical outcomes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r29">[29]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-120608.s10" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Zollinger-Ellison syndrome is caused by gastrinomas that secrete gastrin ectopically. Approximately 80% to 90% of these tumors are located in the &#x0201c;gastrinoma triangle,&#x0201d; which includes the 1st and 2nd portions of the duodenum, the head and neck of the pancreas, and the bile duct distal to the cystic duct takeoff. Excessive gastric acid secretion results in refractory acid reflux and peptic ulcers in the postbulbar duodenum and jejunum. Histopathologic examination demonstrates parietal cell hyperplasia.</p>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">H pylori</italic> gastritis presents with gastritis, duodenitis, gastric ulcers, or duodenal ulcers without other findings characteristic of M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease, and testing for <italic toggle="yes">H pylori</italic> is positive. Hypertrophic lymphocytic gastritis closely resembles M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease but is distinguished histologically by severe inflammation with a predominant intraepithelial lymphocytic infiltrate. Hypertrophic hypersecretory gastropathy involves hypersecretion from all glandular cell types of the stomach, including acid, pepsin, and mucin, with histologic evidence of foveolar and oxyntic gland hyperplasia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r30">[30]</xref></p>
        <p>Gastric polyps are typically more focal, may be numerous, and can mimic M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease. Gastric adenocarcinoma is differentiated pathologically by dysplasia, malignant features, and architectural loss. Gastric lymphoma is identified through biopsy. Infectious etiologies, including histoplasmosis, syphilis, and tuberculosis, and infiltrative disorders such as sarcoidosis, should also be considered in the differential diagnosis.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-120608.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>In children, M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease is typically associated with CMV infection and is self-limited. Resolution occurs spontaneously over a period ranging from several weeks to months. Symptomatic improvement has been reported following CMV treatment with ganciclovir.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-120608.r31">[31]</xref> In adults, the disease generally follows a progressive course. Symptom resolution has been observed in a limited number of adult cases after eradication of <italic toggle="yes">H pylori</italic>. Patients with M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease carry an increased risk of malignancy, primarily gastric carcinoma and gastric lymphoma. Endoscopic surveillance is recommended by some experts for early detection of dysplasia or carcinoma.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-120608.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>In adults, M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease follows a progressive course and may be associated with substantial morbidity and mortality. Complications commonly include malignant transformation, thromboembolic events, and gastric outlet obstruction. Surgical resection of the stomach carries additional risks, including anastomotic leak, fistula formation, and hemorrhage.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-120608.s13" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>Referral to a gastroenterologist or surgical endoscopist is required to obtain biopsies for definitive diagnosis of M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease, manage symptoms, and conduct ongoing endoscopic surveillance due to the risk of gastric cancer. Surgical consultation is indicated for patients with refractory symptoms when gastrectomy is under consideration or planned. A dietitian should provide guidance on implementing a high-protein diet.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-120608.s14" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Deterrence of complications in M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease emphasizes early recognition, timely referral, and proactive management to prevent progressive protein loss, malnutrition, and potential malignant transformation. Primary care clinicians and gastroenterologists are central in monitoring at-risk patients, identifying infectious triggers such as CMV&#x000a0;and&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">H pylori</italic>, and initiating appropriate eradication or antiviral therapy when indicated. Supportive interventions, including implementation of a high-protein diet, micronutrient replacement, and albumin supplementation, reduce the risk of hypoalbuminemia-related complications.&#x000a0;Pharmacologic therapy or surgical intervention may be employed in refractory cases.</p>
        <p>Patient education is critical to optimize outcomes and engagement in care. Clinicians should inform patients and families about the nature of the disease, common symptoms such as fatigue, epigastric pain, and edema, and the importance of adherence to nutritional and pharmacologic regimens, including a high-protein diet. Education should also emphasize regular endoscopic surveillance, infection management, and prompt reporting of new or worsening symptoms. Equipping patients with knowledge about their condition supports self-care, enhances safety, and facilitates collaboration with the interprofessional healthcare team.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-120608.s15" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>M&#x000e9;n&#x000e9;trier disease is a rare gastric disorder characterized by giant mucosal folds, hypochlorhydria, and protein-losing enteropathy, frequently resulting in hypoalbuminemia. The disease follows a progressive course in adults and is self-limited in children, with potential triggers including CMV and <italic toggle="yes">H pylori</italic> infections. Diagnosis requires endoscopic evaluation with targeted biopsies and laboratory assessment. Management is multifaceted, encompassing infection eradication, pharmacologic therapy, supportive care, and surgical intervention in refractory cases. Primary care clinicians are essential for early recognition and referral to gastroenterology for surveillance and comprehensive evaluation.</p>
        <p>Effective management relies on interprofessional collaboration. Physicians, gastroenterologists, and advanced practitioners coordinate diagnostic and therapeutic decisions, while nurses and pharmacists ensure monitoring and medication management. Dietitians provide nutritional support to mitigate protein loss, and pathologists contribute critical histologic interpretation. Open communication, accurate documentation, and shared decision-making enhance patient-centered care, optimize outcomes, and maintain safety throughout the continuum of care.</p>
      </sec>
